  "slug": "models-agent-level-3abc",
  "summary": "Noisy single-cell and microbiome time series now demand likelihood methods that handle latent stochastic dynamics and irregular sampling.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is inference of unobserved stochastic dynamics from noisy, incomplete biological data, with emphasis on likelihood-based estimation. A review covers stochastic gene expression models for growing and dividing cells, including cell-cycle fluctuation signatures and cyclo-stationary behavior. Two preprints address computational tractability: a frequency-domain route makes measurement likelihoods approximately Gaussian to accelerate Bayesian identification of reaction networks, and a latent-state maximum-likelihood method treats stochastic logistic and Lotka-Volterra diffusions observed through irregular microbiome time series. A further preprint infers division control parameters in E. coli from single-cell size trajectories under nonsteady growth. The published PINN framework extends this inverse-problem approach to size-structured fish oogenesis, inferring growth, renewal and recruitment functions from cross-sectional data. What distinguishes these items is their handling of cell-to-cell and transient variation directly within the inference pipeline.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-11"
}
